window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2024-06-2", "source": "2024年06月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose your university is seeking students’ opinions on whether university sports facilities should be open to the public. You are now to write an essay to express your view. You will have 30 minutes for the task. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "In my view, university sports facilities should be opened to the public rather than kept behind closed gates. In\nthe following paragraphs I will give my reasons.\nTo begin with, these facilities are usually built with public funding, yet they stand empty on weekends and\nholidays. Opening them turns a wasted resource into a shared benefit for taxpayers. What is more, many\ncommunities lack affordable places to exercise, so free access to a campus track or gym would clearly improve\nresidents' health. Admittedly, some people worry about campus safety and extra costs. Nevertheless, these\nproblems can be eased by fixed opening hours, an entry registration and a small fee.\nTo sum up, I firmly believe that universities should open their sports facilities to the public. It is advisable that\nthey first set up proper management, so that both students and residents can enjoy the resource in harmony."}, "translation": {"prompt": "农历 (the lunar calendar) 起源于数千年前的中国，根据太阳和月亮的运行规律制定。长期以来，农历在农业生产和人们日常生活中发挥着重要作用。古人依据农历记录日期、安排农活，以便最有效地利用自然资源和气候条件，提高农作物的产量和质量。中国的春节、中秋节等传统节日的日期都基于农历。农历是中国传统文化的重要组成部分，当今依然广为使用。", "sample": "The lunar calendar originated in China thousands of years ago and was formulated based on the movements of the sun and the moon. For a long time, it has played an important role in agricultural production and people's daily life. In the past, ancient people recorded dates and arranged farm work according to the lunar calendar, in order to make the most effective use of natural resources and climatic conditions, thereby improving the yield and quality of crops. The dates of China's traditional festivals, such as the Spring Festival and the Mid-Autumn Festival, are based on the lunar calendar. As an important part of traditional Chinese culture, the lunar calendar is still widely used today."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2024-06-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "A team of researchers led by Priyanka Joshi examined the degree to which men and women relied on \"communicative abstraction\" to verbally convey their ideas and emotions. Communicative abstraction, according to the researchers, reflects the tendency of people to use \"abstract speech that focuses on the broader picture and  __26__  purpose of action rather than concrete speech focusing on details and the means of  __27__  action.\" Interestingly, they found that men were far more likely to speak in the abstract than were women.\nTo arrive at this  __28__  , the researchers examined the linguistic(语言的) patterns of men and women in over 600,000 blog posts written on websites. To do this, the researchers computed abstractness ratings for  __29__  40,000 commonly used words in the English language. Words considered to be concrete could be easily visualized, such as \"table\" or \"chair\". Words that were more  __30__  to visualize, for example, \"justice\" or \"morality\" were considered to be more abstract. They found that men used  __31__  more abstract language in their blog posts.\nWhat is the  __32__  of this effect? The researchers suggest that power differences between the genders—that is, men having more power in society—might be a key determinant(决定因素). For instance, in a follow-up study conducted with a sample of 300 students, the researchers  __33__  power dynamics in an interpersonal setting to see if this would influence communicative abstraction. They found that participants in a high-power interviewer role were more likely to give abstract descriptions of behaviors than were participants in a low-power interviewee role. This suggests that communicating more abstractly does not reflect a  __34__  tendency of men or women but rather  __35__  within specific contexts.", "matchIntro": "How to better work towards long-term goals", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Hal Hershfield, a psychologist at the UCLA Anderson School of Management, wanted to know why people weren't saving for retirement. Across the board, people are living longer. Logically, they'll need more money to live comfortably in their post-work years. And yet, savings rates in the U.S. have gone down in recent decades, not up."}, {"label": "B", "text": "To help explain this seemingly irrational behavior, Hershfield and his team scanned the brains of study participants while asking them to what degree various traits(特征)—like \"honorable\" or \"funny\"—applied to their current self, their future self, a current other, or a future other. As participants answered, Hershfield's team recorded which parts of their brains lit up. Unsurprisingly, people's brains were most active when thinking about their current selves and least active when thinking about a current other. But the team found that participants' brain activity while considering their future selves more closely resembled their brain activity while thinking about a current other rather than the current self."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Put in practical terms, when thinking of yourself in a month or a year or a decade, your brain registers that person in ways similar to how it would register Taylor Swift or the mailman. Understood in that way, saving for retirement is the equivalent of giving money away to someone else entirely."}, {"label": "D", "text": "In light of Hershfield's study, one simple question arises: Is it possible to make our present selves give a damn about our future selves? The answers are anything but simple."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Seen through the lens of the present self versus the future self, our self-defeating actions—like choosing to watch television rather than go to the gym—suddenly make perfect sense. We get to enjoy the very concrete, immediate benefits of our actions while someone else (namely, our future selves) suffers the hypothetical(假设的), far-off consequences. As a result, the decisions we make for our present selves often look very different from our decisions for our future selves. We believe that tomorrow will be different. We believe that we will be different tomorrow; but in doing so, we prioritize our current mood over the consequences of our inaction for the future self."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Understanding our procrastination(拖延) through the lens of the present and future selves, we're left with three possible solutions: The first is to force your future self to do whatever your present self doesn't want to do. The second is to convince your present self that your future self is, in fact, still you. If the central problem is that we think of our future selves as other people, it follows that trying to identify more closely with our future selves will encourage us to make better long-term decisions."}, {"label": "G", "text": "In a follow-up study, Hershfield wanted to explore ways to bridge the disconnect between the present and future selves and encourage people to save more for retirement. He and his team took photos of study participants, and then used image processing to visually age their faces. Participants were then placed in a virtual reality setting where they could look into a mirror and see their aged selves looking back at them. Participants who saw their aged selves said they would save 30% more of their salary for retirement than the control group."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Whatever your long-term goals may be—getting in better shape, launching your own business, writing a book—thinking about your deadline in terms of days rather than months or years can help you wrap your mind around how close the future really is."}, {"label": "I", "text": "The third solution is to forget about your future self and use your present self's love of instant gratification(满足感) to your advantage. While the two tactics(手段) above can be effective in making better long-term choices, in the end, you're still struggling against human nature. Our brains are hard-wired for instant gratification. Instead of fighting your present self's need for immediate rewards, why not use it to your advantage? When most of us set goals, we focus on long-term results we want to see—e.g., losing weight, getting a promotion, retiring in comfort, etc. While those visions of our future selves can be inspiring, when it comes to actually doing the day-to-day work, it may be more effective to reframe activities in terms of their immediate, or at least very near-term, rewards."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Take writing this article, for instance. It's easy for me to imagine how amazing it will feel at the end of the workday to have this article done. This isn't just my opinion. Research partners Kaitlin Woolley of Cornell University and Ayelet Fishbach of the University of Chicago have made a career out of studying the differences between the goals that people achieve and the ones that fall to the wayside."}, {"label": "K", "text": "\"In one study, we asked people online about the goals they set at the beginning of the year. Most people set goals to achieve delayed, long-term benefits, such as career advancement, debt repayment, or improved health. We asked these individuals how enjoyable it was to pursue their goal, as well as how important their goal was. We also asked whether they were still working on their goals two months after setting them. We found that enjoyment predicted people's goal persistence two months after setting the goal far more than how important they rated their goal to be,\" Woolley said."}, {"label": "L", "text": "This pattern held true across a wide variety of goals from exercising to studying to eating healthier foods. For example, people ate 50% more of a healthy food when directed to focus on the good taste rather than the long-term health benefits. Other studies have shown a greater uptake of exercise in people who were told to think of the enjoyment of doing the exercise now rather than future health gains."}, {"label": "M", "text": "These findings suggest that when it comes to achieving your goals, enjoying the process itself is more important than wanting the long-term benefits. In other words, present self trumps(战胜) future self. Who says instant gratification has to be a bad thing? By all means, set ambitious long-term goals for your future self, but when it comes to actually following through day-to-day, make sure your present self knows what's in it for her too."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "People often wonder why some entrepreneurs have greater success than others. Is it habits, connections, luck, work ethic or any other behavior? I believe the key to success is willpower. Willpower is the ability to control yourself. It is a strong determination that allows you to do something difficult. It is a behavior we are born with more than one we learn; however, it is possible to not only learn it, but also strengthen it with constant exercise.\nWillpower is just like a muscle; to keep it strong you need to constantly exercise it. People with a great amount of willpower have the discipline to develop positive, successful habits. Even with an incredible amount of talent, without the discipline and motivation to create positive habits, it can be difficult to achieve success.\nWillpower and habits go hand in hand. It is critical to create good habits and take the actions necessary to stick to those habits day in and day out for greater success. Almost half of our daily actions are part of our habits and not decisions, so once the correct habits are in place, you will automatically perform those tasks on a day-to-day basis.\nThe best way to create and stick to a habit is to have strong motivation. It's easier to change your habits to lose weight if you have a health issue and you want to be around longer for your kids, or if your business is something you're passionate about. Having a valuable outcome associated with a habit will help you stick to that habit permanently.\nThe art of self-control is one that most successful individuals have mastered. Self-control enables you to avoid behaviors that don't contribute to your success and adopt those that do.\nBecause there is a delayed satisfaction associated with self-control, it can be easy to get off track. However, if you work on sticking to those small positive habits one day at a time, it becomes easier to stay strong and achieve that delayed reward. Once a reward is achieved, it is much easier to continue sticking to your habits.", "47": "People often wonder why some entrepreneurs have greater success than others. Is it habits, connections, luck, work ethic or any other behavior? I believe the key to success is willpower. Willpower is the ability to control yourself. It is a strong determination that allows you to do something difficult. It is a behavior we are born with more than one we learn; however, it is possible to not only learn it, but also strengthen it with constant exercise.\nWillpower is just like a muscle; to keep it strong you need to constantly exercise it. People with a great amount of willpower have the discipline to develop positive, successful habits. Even with an incredible amount of talent, without the discipline and motivation to create positive habits, it can be difficult to achieve success.\nWillpower and habits go hand in hand. It is critical to create good habits and take the actions necessary to stick to those habits day in and day out for greater success. Almost half of our daily actions are part of our habits and not decisions, so once the correct habits are in place, you will automatically perform those tasks on a day-to-day basis.\nThe best way to create and stick to a habit is to have strong motivation. It's easier to change your habits to lose weight if you have a health issue and you want to be around longer for your kids, or if your business is something you're passionate about. Having a valuable outcome associated with a habit will help you stick to that habit permanently.\nThe art of self-control is one that most successful individuals have mastered. Self-control enables you to avoid behaviors that don't contribute to your success and adopt those that do.\nBecause there is a delayed satisfaction associated with self-control, it can be easy to get off track. However, if you work on sticking to those small positive habits one day at a time, it becomes easier to stay strong and achieve that delayed reward. Once a reward is achieved, it is much easier to continue sticking to your habits.", "48": "People often wonder why some entrepreneurs have greater success than others. Is it habits, connections, luck, work ethic or any other behavior? I believe the key to success is willpower. Willpower is the ability to control yourself. It is a strong determination that allows you to do something difficult. It is a behavior we are born with more than one we learn; however, it is possible to not only learn it, but also strengthen it with constant exercise.\nWillpower is just like a muscle; to keep it strong you need to constantly exercise it. People with a great amount of willpower have the discipline to develop positive, successful habits. Even with an incredible amount of talent, without the discipline and motivation to create positive habits, it can be difficult to achieve success.\nWillpower and habits go hand in hand. It is critical to create good habits and take the actions necessary to stick to those habits day in and day out for greater success. Almost half of our daily actions are part of our habits and not decisions, so once the correct habits are in place, you will automatically perform those tasks on a day-to-day basis.\nThe best way to create and stick to a habit is to have strong motivation. It's easier to change your habits to lose weight if you have a health issue and you want to be around longer for your kids, or if your business is something you're passionate about. Having a valuable outcome associated with a habit will help you stick to that habit permanently.\nThe art of self-control is one that most successful individuals have mastered. Self-control enables you to avoid behaviors that don't contribute to your success and adopt those that do.\nBecause there is a delayed satisfaction associated with self-control, it can be easy to get off track. However, if you work on sticking to those small positive habits one day at a time, it becomes easier to stay strong and achieve that delayed reward. Once a reward is achieved, it is much easier to continue sticking to your habits.", "49": "People often wonder why some entrepreneurs have greater success than others. Is it habits, connections, luck, work ethic or any other behavior? I believe the key to success is willpower. Willpower is the ability to control yourself. It is a strong determination that allows you to do something difficult. It is a behavior we are born with more than one we learn; however, it is possible to not only learn it, but also strengthen it with constant exercise.\nWillpower is just like a muscle; to keep it strong you need to constantly exercise it. People with a great amount of willpower have the discipline to develop positive, successful habits. Even with an incredible amount of talent, without the discipline and motivation to create positive habits, it can be difficult to achieve success.\nWillpower and habits go hand in hand. It is critical to create good habits and take the actions necessary to stick to those habits day in and day out for greater success. Almost half of our daily actions are part of our habits and not decisions, so once the correct habits are in place, you will automatically perform those tasks on a day-to-day basis.\nThe best way to create and stick to a habit is to have strong motivation. It's easier to change your habits to lose weight if you have a health issue and you want to be around longer for your kids, or if your business is something you're passionate about. Having a valuable outcome associated with a habit will help you stick to that habit permanently.\nThe art of self-control is one that most successful individuals have mastered. Self-control enables you to avoid behaviors that don't contribute to your success and adopt those that do.\nBecause there is a delayed satisfaction associated with self-control, it can be easy to get off track. However, if you work on sticking to those small positive habits one day at a time, it becomes easier to stay strong and achieve that delayed reward. Once a reward is achieved, it is much easier to continue sticking to your habits.", "50": "People often wonder why some entrepreneurs have greater success than others. Is it habits, connections, luck, work ethic or any other behavior? I believe the key to success is willpower. Willpower is the ability to control yourself. It is a strong determination that allows you to do something difficult. It is a behavior we are born with more than one we learn; however, it is possible to not only learn it, but also strengthen it with constant exercise.\nWillpower is just like a muscle; to keep it strong you need to constantly exercise it. People with a great amount of willpower have the discipline to develop positive, successful habits. Even with an incredible amount of talent, without the discipline and motivation to create positive habits, it can be difficult to achieve success.\nWillpower and habits go hand in hand. It is critical to create good habits and take the actions necessary to stick to those habits day in and day out for greater success. Almost half of our daily actions are part of our habits and not decisions, so once the correct habits are in place, you will automatically perform those tasks on a day-to-day basis.\nThe best way to create and stick to a habit is to have strong motivation. It's easier to change your habits to lose weight if you have a health issue and you want to be around longer for your kids, or if your business is something you're passionate about. Having a valuable outcome associated with a habit will help you stick to that habit permanently.\nThe art of self-control is one that most successful individuals have mastered. Self-control enables you to avoid behaviors that don't contribute to your success and adopt those that do.\nBecause there is a delayed satisfaction associated with self-control, it can be easy to get off track. However, if you work on sticking to those small positive habits one day at a time, it becomes easier to stay strong and achieve that delayed reward. Once a reward is achieved, it is much easier to continue sticking to your habits.", "51": "Today, most scientific research is funded by government grants, companies doing research and development, and non-profit foundations. As a society, we reap the rewards from this science, but we also help pay for it. You indirectly support science through taxes you pay, products and services you purchase, and donations you make.\nFunding for science has changed with the times. Historically, science has been largely supported through private patronage(资助), church sponsorship, or simply paying for the research yourself. Today, researchers are likely to be funded by a mix of grants from various government agencies, institutions, and foundations. Other research is funded by private companies. Such corporate sponsorship is widespread in some fields. Almost 75% of U.S. clinical trials in medicine are paid for by private companies. And, of course, some researchers today still fund small-scale studies out of their own pockets. Most of us can't afford to do nuclear research as a private hobby, but birdwatchers, rock collectors, and others can do real research on a limited budget.\nIn a perfect world, money wouldn't matter—all scientific studies would be completely objective. But in the real world, funding may introduce biases. Drug research sponsored by the pharmaceutical(制药的) industry is more likely to end up favoring the drug under consideration than studies sponsored by government grants or charitable organizations. Similarly, nutrition research sponsored by the food industry is more likely to end up favoring the food under consideration than independently funded research.\nSo what should we make of all this? Should we ignore any research funded by companies or special interest groups? Certainly not. These groups provide invaluable funding for scientific research. Furthermore, science has many safeguards in place to catch instances of bias that affect research outcomes. Ultimately, misleading results will be corrected as science proceeds; however, this process takes time. Meanwhile, it pays to examine studies funded by industry or special interest groups with extra care. Are the results consistent with other independently funded studies? What do other scientists have to say about this research? A little examination can go a long way towards identifying bias associated with the funding source.", "52": "Today, most scientific research is funded by government grants, companies doing research and development, and non-profit foundations. As a society, we reap the rewards from this science, but we also help pay for it. You indirectly support science through taxes you pay, products and services you purchase, and donations you make.\nFunding for science has changed with the times. Historically, science has been largely supported through private patronage(资助), church sponsorship, or simply paying for the research yourself. Today, researchers are likely to be funded by a mix of grants from various government agencies, institutions, and foundations. Other research is funded by private companies. Such corporate sponsorship is widespread in some fields. Almost 75% of U.S. clinical trials in medicine are paid for by private companies. And, of course, some researchers today still fund small-scale studies out of their own pockets. Most of us can't afford to do nuclear research as a private hobby, but birdwatchers, rock collectors, and others can do real research on a limited budget.\nIn a perfect world, money wouldn't matter—all scientific studies would be completely objective. But in the real world, funding may introduce biases. Drug research sponsored by the pharmaceutical(制药的) industry is more likely to end up favoring the drug under consideration than studies sponsored by government grants or charitable organizations. Similarly, nutrition research sponsored by the food industry is more likely to end up favoring the food under consideration than independently funded research.\nSo what should we make of all this? Should we ignore any research funded by companies or special interest groups? Certainly not. These groups provide invaluable funding for scientific research. Furthermore, science has many safeguards in place to catch instances of bias that affect research outcomes. Ultimately, misleading results will be corrected as science proceeds; however, this process takes time. Meanwhile, it pays to examine studies funded by industry or special interest groups with extra care. Are the results consistent with other independently funded studies? What do other scientists have to say about this research? A little examination can go a long way towards identifying bias associated with the funding source.", "53": "Today, most scientific research is funded by government grants, companies doing research and development, and non-profit foundations. As a society, we reap the rewards from this science, but we also help pay for it. You indirectly support science through taxes you pay, products and services you purchase, and donations you make.\nFunding for science has changed with the times. Historically, science has been largely supported through private patronage(资助), church sponsorship, or simply paying for the research yourself. Today, researchers are likely to be funded by a mix of grants from various government agencies, institutions, and foundations. Other research is funded by private companies. Such corporate sponsorship is widespread in some fields. Almost 75% of U.S. clinical trials in medicine are paid for by private companies. And, of course, some researchers today still fund small-scale studies out of their own pockets. Most of us can't afford to do nuclear research as a private hobby, but birdwatchers, rock collectors, and others can do real research on a limited budget.\nIn a perfect world, money wouldn't matter—all scientific studies would be completely objective. But in the real world, funding may introduce biases. Drug research sponsored by the pharmaceutical(制药的) industry is more likely to end up favoring the drug under consideration than studies sponsored by government grants or charitable organizations. Similarly, nutrition research sponsored by the food industry is more likely to end up favoring the food under consideration than independently funded research.\nSo what should we make of all this? Should we ignore any research funded by companies or special interest groups? Certainly not. These groups provide invaluable funding for scientific research. Furthermore, science has many safeguards in place to catch instances of bias that affect research outcomes. Ultimately, misleading results will be corrected as science proceeds; however, this process takes time. Meanwhile, it pays to examine studies funded by industry or special interest groups with extra care. Are the results consistent with other independently funded studies? What do other scientists have to say about this research? A little examination can go a long way towards identifying bias associated with the funding source.", "54": "Today, most scientific research is funded by government grants, companies doing research and development, and non-profit foundations. As a society, we reap the rewards from this science, but we also help pay for it. You indirectly support science through taxes you pay, products and services you purchase, and donations you make.\nFunding for science has changed with the times. Historically, science has been largely supported through private patronage(资助), church sponsorship, or simply paying for the research yourself. Today, researchers are likely to be funded by a mix of grants from various government agencies, institutions, and foundations. Other research is funded by private companies. Such corporate sponsorship is widespread in some fields. Almost 75% of U.S. clinical trials in medicine are paid for by private companies. And, of course, some researchers today still fund small-scale studies out of their own pockets. Most of us can't afford to do nuclear research as a private hobby, but birdwatchers, rock collectors, and others can do real research on a limited budget.\nIn a perfect world, money wouldn't matter—all scientific studies would be completely objective. But in the real world, funding may introduce biases. Drug research sponsored by the pharmaceutical(制药的) industry is more likely to end up favoring the drug under consideration than studies sponsored by government grants or charitable organizations. Similarly, nutrition research sponsored by the food industry is more likely to end up favoring the food under consideration than independently funded research.\nSo what should we make of all this? Should we ignore any research funded by companies or special interest groups? Certainly not. These groups provide invaluable funding for scientific research. Furthermore, science has many safeguards in place to catch instances of bias that affect research outcomes. Ultimately, misleading results will be corrected as science proceeds; however, this process takes time. Meanwhile, it pays to examine studies funded by industry or special interest groups with extra care. Are the results consistent with other independently funded studies? What do other scientists have to say about this research? A little examination can go a long way towards identifying bias associated with the funding source.", "55": "Today, most scientific research is funded by government grants, companies doing research and development, and non-profit foundations. As a society, we reap the rewards from this science, but we also help pay for it. You indirectly support science through taxes you pay, products and services you purchase, and donations you make.\nFunding for science has changed with the times. Historically, science has been largely supported through private patronage(资助), church sponsorship, or simply paying for the research yourself. Today, researchers are likely to be funded by a mix of grants from various government agencies, institutions, and foundations. Other research is funded by private companies. Such corporate sponsorship is widespread in some fields. Almost 75% of U.S. clinical trials in medicine are paid for by private companies. And, of course, some researchers today still fund small-scale studies out of their own pockets. Most of us can't afford to do nuclear research as a private hobby, but birdwatchers, rock collectors, and others can do real research on a limited budget.\nIn a perfect world, money wouldn't matter—all scientific studies would be completely objective. But in the real world, funding may introduce biases. Drug research sponsored by the pharmaceutical(制药的) industry is more likely to end up favoring the drug under consideration than studies sponsored by government grants or charitable organizations. Similarly, nutrition research sponsored by the food industry is more likely to end up favoring the food under consideration than independently funded research.\nSo what should we make of all this? Should we ignore any research funded by companies or special interest groups? Certainly not. These groups provide invaluable funding for scientific research. Furthermore, science has many safeguards in place to catch instances of bias that affect research outcomes. Ultimately, misleading results will be corrected as science proceeds; however, this process takes time. Meanwhile, it pays to examine studies funded by industry or special interest groups with extra care. Are the results consistent with other independently funded studies? What do other scientists have to say about this research? A little examination can go a long way towards identifying bias associated with the funding source."}, "questions": [{"id": "2024-06-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn about the JetBlue Airlines flight from the news report?", "options": ["A) It hit a bird shortly after takeoff.", "B) Its crew members went on strike.", "C) It narrowly escaped a plane crash when turning around.", "D) Its captain got slightly injured during the forced landing."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It hit a bird shortly after takeoff.\n【定位】A JetBlue Airlines flight from West Palm Beach to New York City was forced to turn around\nand land Sunday morning after the plane struck a bird.\n周日上午，一架从西棕榈滩飞往纽约市的捷蓝航空航班在撞上一只鸟之后，被迫调头返航\n降落。\n【信号】新闻首句即事件核心。答案就在录音第一句 was forced to turn around and land Sunday\nmorning after the plane struck a bird：新闻报道往往开篇一句话就把 \"谁、发生了什\n么、为什么\" 交代清楚，这句的 after 引出原因（撞鸟），正是承载答案的核心句。\n【替换】after the plane struck a bird ↔ It hit a bird\nstruck 换成 hit\nwas forced to turn around and land Sunday morning ↔ shortly after takeoff\n返航即起飞后不久\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIts crew members went on strike 说机组罢工，录音通篇只字未提任何罢工，返航原因明\n确是撞鸟，属于无中生有。\nC) 过度推断\nIt narrowly escaped a plane crash when turning around 盗用了录音里真出现的 turn\naround（返航）来制造熟悉感，但录音只说返航降落、明说 No injuries were reported，\n根本没提 \"险些坠机\"，是把普通返航夸大成濒临坠机。\n借用原文：turned around just minutes after takeoff\nD) 与原文相反\nIts captain got slightly injured during the forced landing 借用了录音真有的 forced to\nturn around and land（被迫降落）来骗耳朵，但录音紧接着明说 No injuries were\nreported on the plane——没有任何人受伤，更没说机长受伤，与原文正好相反。\n借用原文：No injuries were reported on the plane", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "How did the passengers feel when the plane came to a stop, according to Brian Healy?", "options": ["A) Panic.", "B) Nervous.", "C) Relieved.", "D) Contented."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度观点题） Relieved.\n【定位】\"There was total quiet, and then there was relief when the plane came to a stop.\"\n\"当时一片寂静，随后在飞机停稳的那一刻，大家都松了一口气。\"\n【信号】题干时间词对准原文。录音里 there was relief when the plane came to a stop 这句的时\n间状语 when the plane came to a stop 与题干一字不差，时间点对上，这句就是答案\n句；情绪词是 relief（松了口气）。\n【替换】there was relief ↔ Relieved\n名词relief转形容词Relieved\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nPanic 盗用了录音真说过的 a split second of panic，但那是描述事发瞬间（split\nsecond）的情绪，不是题干问的 \"飞机停稳时\"，时间点错位。\n借用原文：a split second of panic\nB) 答非所问\nNervous 盗用了录音的 this nervous reaction on the plane，但那形容的是事件过程中的\n紧张反应，同样不是 \"飞机停稳时\" 的感受，时间点不符。\n借用原文：this nervous reaction on the plane\nD) 未提及\nContented（满足）和正确项 relief 意思相近，是赌你分不清两个正面情绪词，但录音从头\n到尾只说了 relief，从没出现 \"满足\" 这层意思，属于无中生有。\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 A deadly snake, which had finally been tracked down after escaping a zoo, has slipped away for\nthe second time. The poisonous snake forced the closure of the attraction last week when staff noticed\nthe disappearance.\n一条致命的毒蛇在从动物园逃脱后终于被追踪到,却第二次溜走了。上周当工作人员注意到这条毒蛇失踪\n时,它迫使该景点关闭。\n2 After six days of desperate searching, he was eventually found and placed in a supposedly secure area.\nBut it seems the animal is no fan of the zoo, because yet again he is out on the loose. The snake is a\nrelative newcomer to the zoo but has already been frustrating its staff. The staff believe he squeezed\nhimself out of a gap located around new energy-saving bulbs installed inside the snake house. He\nwas only found the first time around because staff brought in a special machine to trace him inside a\nwall opening.\n经过六天的拼命搜寻,它最终被找到并被安置在一个据称安全的区域。但这只动物似乎并不喜欢这家动物\n园,因为它再次逃脱了。这条蛇是动物园相对较新的成员,但已经让工作人员感到沮丧。工作人员认为它是\n从蛇舍内安装的新型节能灯泡周围的缝隙中挤出去的。它第一次被找到是因为工作人员带来了一台特殊的\n机器,在墙壁开口内追踪到了它。\n3 This kind of snake is one of the most deadly and poisonous in the wild, and they can range from 3 to\n5 meters in length.\n这种蛇是野外最致命、毒性最强的蛇类之一,体长可达3至5米。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What do we learn about the deadly snake from the news report?", "options": ["A) He is now kept in a secure area.", "B) He has escaped the zoo once again.", "C) He has been caught a second time.", "D) He finally disappeared six days ago."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） He has escaped the zoo once again.\n【定位】A deadly snake, which had finally been tracked down after escaping a zoo, has slipped away\nfor the second time.\n一条致命的蛇，在逃出动物园后终于被找回，如今又第二次溜走了。\n【信号】开篇首句即落点。新闻的核心事实常压在第一句。这里 has slipped away for the second\ntime（第二次溜走了）就是承载答案的信号句——它点明这条蛇\"再次逃跑\"这一最新状态。\n【替换】has slipped away for the second time ↔ has escaped the zoo once again\nslip away=escape，second time=once again\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 placed in a supposedly secure area（被放进所谓安全区域），但紧接着就\n是 yet again he is out on the loose——它早跑出来了。A 盗用 secure area 这个原词，\n描述的却是已被推翻的\"上一个阶段\"状态，不是它\"现在\"的状况。\n借用原文：placed in a supposedly secure area\nC) 与原文相反\n录音里确有 tracked down 和 found（被找到/被抓）以及 second time 的字眼，C 用\ncaught a second time 把这些原词拼起来。但 second time 在录音里是修饰\"逃跑\"的\n（slipped away for the second time），抓只发生过一次。C 把动作方向从\"逃\"偷换\n成\"抓\"，与原文相反。\n借用原文：tracked down after escaping a zoo, has slipped away for the second time\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音的 After six days of desperate searching（搜寻了六天）里确有 six days，D 便拿\nsix days ago 来骗。但六天指的是\"工作人员搜寻它花的时长\"，不是它\"六天前消失\"；而且\n它现在是又跑了、不是\"finally disappeared（终于消失）\"。数字对得上、事却对不上。\n借用原文：After six days of desperate searching", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "How have the zoo staff been feeling about the snake?", "options": ["A) Squeezed.", "B) Threatened.", "C) Disappointed.", "D) Frustrated."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） Frustrated.\n【定位】The snake is a relative newcomer to the zoo but has already been frustrating its staff.\n这条蛇到动物园不算久，却已经让它的工作人员伤透了脑筋（备受挫败）。\n【信号】情绪动词 frustrating。答案信号就是那个直接表达感受的词 frustrating its staff（让工作\n人员受挫/懊恼）。态度题的落点往往就是这样一个带感情色彩的形容词或动词，听到它就\n锁定。\n【替换】has already been frustrating its staff ↔ Frustrated\n主动 frustrating→被动 frustrated\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里 he squeezed himself out of a gap（蛇把自己挤出缝隙）确有 squeezed，A 就拿\n这个原词来骗。但 squeezed 讲的是蛇\"怎么逃出去的\"动作，根本不是工作人员的\"感受\"，\n与题目问的 feeling 完全是两码事。\n借用原文：he squeezed himself out of a gap\nB) 未提及\n这条蛇虽 deadly（致命），但录音从没说工作人员\"感到受威胁\"。Threatened 是顺着\"毒\n蛇危险\"自行脑补出来的情绪，录音里并无对应的词，属无中生有。\nC) 偷换概念\nDisappointed（失望）与正确的 Frustrated（受挫/懊恼）都是负面情绪、含义相邻，最难\n切开。但录音明明白白念的是 frustrating，不是 disappointing。C 用一个意思相近却并非\n原文的情绪词来顶替，差在\"不是录音说的那个词\"。\n借用原文：has already been frustrating its staff", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we learn from the news report about the introduction of electric bikes into Jacksonville?", "options": ["A) It is condemned as a crazy idea.", "B) It is enriching the city's night life.", "C) It is questioned by local residents.", "D) It is giving rise to safety concerns."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is giving rise to safety concerns.\n【定位】But they're leading to safety concerns, mainly at night when some riders don't follow the rules\nof the road.\n但它们正在引发安全担忧，主要发生在夜里，一些骑车人不遵守交通规则的时候。\n【信号】转折 But 之后才是真意。答案藏在转折词后面：第 1 段先说电动车很火 Electric bikes\nhave been the craze，紧接一个 But they're leading to safety concerns。新闻里 But 一\n转，前面的火爆是铺垫，转折后的安全担忧才是报道真正要讲的落点。\n【替换】they're leading to safety concerns ↔ It is giving rise to safety concerns.\nlead to = give rise to\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音说电动车 have been the craze（很火爆、风靡），A 项 condemned as a crazy idea\n把 craze 这个原词联想成「crazy／疯狂主意被谴责」。craze（风靡）和 crazy（疯狂）发\n音相近但意思相反，录音根本没有谴责之意，是听音近词就选的陷阱。\n借用原文：Electric bikes have been the craze\nB) 张冠李戴\nenriching the city's night life 听着像呼应录音里夜里、城市等词，但录音第 4 段说的是引\n进电动车是为了 bring new life into downtown（带来新活力，那是目的／初衷），并没\n说它正在丰富夜生活。把第 4 段的目的安到第 5 题的现状评价上，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：bring new life into downtown\nC) 未提及\nquestioned by local residents 里的「被本地居民质疑」在录音里找不到落点：报道讲的是\nsafety concerns（安全担忧），并没有出现 local residents 质疑这一方。属于无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What are electric bike riders supposed to do?", "options": ["A) Avoid entering one-way streets.", "B) Ensure the safety of pedestrians.", "C) Follow all the traffic rules drivers do.", "D) Give way to automobiles at all times."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Follow all the traffic rules drivers do.\n【定位】Electric bike riders have to follow all the same rules as you would if you were in\nan automobile.\n电动车骑车人必须遵守和你开汽车时一样的全部规则。\n【信号】义务句 have to 即落点。第 3 段 have to follow all the same rules 里的 have to（必\n须）是明确的义务标志。预读时就定了「听 have to／must」，这句一出现就是答案句。\n【替换】follow all the same rules as you would if you were in an automobile ↔\nFollow all the traffic rules drivers do.\nin an automobile = drivers do\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nAvoid entering one-way streets 盗用了录音 one-way streets 这个原词，但录音说的是\n「不要在单行道上逆行（traveling in the opposite direction）」，不是「避免进入单行\n道」。它只截取了规则里的一个例子并改了意思，答非所问——题目问的是总要求，不是某\n一条细则。\n借用原文：traveling in the opposite direction of traffic on one-way streets\nB) 张冠李戴\nEnsure the safety of pedestrians 里的 pedestrians 借自第 2 段 pedestrian paths。但录\n音从没要求骑车人「保证行人安全」；第 4 段只说骑车人和司机都要 paying attention\n（注意安全）。把注意夸成保证行人安全，且 pedestrian 一词出处也不对，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：on pedestrian paths\nD) 原词陷阱\nGive way to automobiles at all times 借了录音的 automobile 一词，但录音说骑车人要\n遵守「和开汽车一样的规则」，是平等守规，绝没有「随时给汽车让路」的意思。at all\ntimes（任何时候）是绝对化的无中生有。\n借用原文：as you would if you were in an automobile", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "Why did City Council member Anna Cumber advocate the introduction of electric bikes into Jacksonville?", "options": ["A) To ease the city's busy traffic.", "B) To bring new life into the city.", "C) To add a new means of transport.", "D) To reduce the city's air pollution."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （目的题） To bring new life into the city.\n【定位】City Council Member Anna Cumber was instrumental in introducing the electric bikes to\nJacksonville as a way to bring new life into downtown.\n市议会议员 Anna Cumber 在把电动车引进杰克逊维尔一事上起了关键作用，目的是给市中\n心带来新的活力。\n【信号】目的状语 as a way to。第 4 段 as a way to bring new life into downtown 里的 as a\nway to（作为……的一种方式）就是明确的目的标志，它后面接的就是 Anna Cumber 引进\n电动车的初衷。预读已锁定听目的结构，这句正中。\n【替换】as a way to bring new life into downtown ↔ To bring new life into the city.\ndowntown = the city\n【排除】A) 未提及\nTo ease the city's busy traffic 听着合理，但录音里 Anna Cumber 引进电动车的目的只说\n了 bring new life into downtown，从没提缓解繁忙交通。属无中生有的合理化推断。\nC) 过度推断\nTo add a new means of transport 看似没错——电动车确实是新交通工具，但录音点明的\n目的是带来新活力，不是增加一种交通方式。这是顺着常识多推一步、偏离原文所说目的。\nD) 未提及\nTo reduce the city's air pollution 引入「空气污染」这个全新概念，录音通篇没出现\npollution 或环保相关内容。属彻底的无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What should we do with the topic of money, according to the woman?", "options": ["A) Take it seriously.", "B) Discuss it regularly.", "C) Make it sound scary.", "D) Talk about it openly."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Discuss it regularly.\n【定位】You should make money a regular topic of discussion.\n你应该让钱成为一个经常讨论的话题。\n【信号】应答方的 should 建议句。男士问\"最好怎么教孩子理财\"，女士开口第一句就是 You\nshould make money a regular topic of discussion，should 是明确的建议信号，被问者\n的应答正是答案落点。\n【替换】a regular topic of discussion ↔ Discuss it regularly\n名词短语改写成动词\n【排除】A) 未提及\nTake it seriously.（认真对待）听起来很顺理成章，但女士没说要\"认真严肃对待\"，反而强\n调要让它不吓人、变本能，属凭常识脑补、原文未提。\nC) 与原文相反\n录音里女士说要让钱\"不是个吓人的话题\" rather than a scary subject，Make it sound\nscary. 盗用了原词 scary 却把意思反了过来，正是\"听到原词就想选\"的陷阱。\n借用原文：rather than a scary subject\nD) 张冠李戴\nTalk about it openly. 对应的 openly 来自男士的话 we talk openly about things，是男士\n说的，而题目问的是 according to the woman（女士），把男士的话安到女士头上，属张\n冠李戴。\n借用原文：we talk openly about things", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "How does the woman say money can be made real for children?", "options": ["A) By shopping around for the best deals.", "B) By making abstract sums relevant.", "C) By citing concrete examples.", "D) By visiting discount stores."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） By citing concrete examples.\n【定位】It's also essential to make money real for children through practical examples. Working out\nhow much we save using discount pizza coupons, for example, is much more relevant than\nabstract sums.\n通过实际的例子让钱对孩子变得真实也很重要。比如算一算我们用披萨折扣券省了多少钱，\n这比抽象的算数有意义得多。\n【信号】for example 引出具体做法。女士说要 through practical examples 并紧接 for example\n举出披萨折扣券的例子，practical examples 加 for example 正是\"用具体例子\"的方式信\n号，承载 How 的答案。\n【替换】through practical examples ↔ By citing concrete examples\n同义词替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nBy shopping around for the best deals. 里 shopping around for the best deals 是男士\n在 how to shop around for the best deals 里说的别的话题，且非\"让钱变真实\"的方法，\n原词照搬却答非所问。\n借用原文：how to shop around for the best deals\nB) 与原文相反\n录音说具体例子\"比抽象算数有意义得多\" much more relevant than abstract sums，\nabstract sums 是被否定、被比下去的一方；By making abstract sums relevant. 盗用这\n两个词反过来说\"让抽象算数变得相关\"，与原文相反。\n借用原文：much more relevant than abstract sums\nD) 偷换概念\nBy visiting discount stores. 里 discount 来自录音的 discount pizza coupons（折扣\n券），但原文是\"用折扣券\"不是\"逛折扣店\"，盗一个 discount 把概念偷换了。\n借用原文：discount pizza coupons", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What is the common practice between the man and the woman?", "options": ["A) Paying their kids to help with housework.", "B) Setting kids a good example by sharing chores.", "C) Giving kids pocket money according to their needs.", "D) Urging kids to deposit some of their gift money."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Paying their kids to help with housework.\n【定位】M: We also give our kids pocket money, and the amount they get is linked to chores, such as\nputting the bins out and emptying the dishwasher. W: We do that too, and it's paid according\nto their age.\n男：我们也给孩子零花钱，金额跟做家务挂钩，比如倒垃圾、清空洗碗机。女：我们也这么\n做，而且是按年龄发的。\n【信号】We do that too 的附和。男士说零花钱\"跟做家务挂钩\" the amount they get is linked\nto chores，女士紧接 We do that too（我们也这么做），这句附和正是\"共同做法\"的信\n号，把男士那件事确认为两人共有。\n【替换】pocket money, and the amount they get is linked to chores ↔\nPaying their kids to help with housework\n挂钩家务即按家务付钱\nWe do that too ↔ 10A\n附和坐实共同点\n【排除】B) 未提及\nSetting kids a good example by sharing chores.（以分担家务给孩子做榜样）听着合\n理，但录音说的是\"让孩子做家务换零花钱\"，不是大人分担家务做榜样，原文并无此意。\nC) 原词陷阱\nGiving kids pocket money according to their needs. 照搬原词 pocket money，但加的\n条件是 according to their needs（按需要），录音里是\"按家务\"和\"按年龄\" it's paid\naccording to their age，条件被偷换。\n借用原文：it's paid according to their age\nD) 张冠李戴\nUrging kids to deposit some of their gift money. 里 deposit、gift money 出自男士单\n方面的 they would go to the branch and deposit their gift money，女士回应说\"我没\n考虑过这么做\"，并非两人共同做法。\n借用原文：they would go to the branch and deposit their gift money", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What is the woman always talking about to her elder daughter?", "options": ["A) The importance of cutting down family expenses.", "B) The need to learn important lessons from her elders.", "C) The delight in seeing their savings grow.", "D) The necessity of saving into a pension."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The necessity of saving into a pension.\n【定位】W: I am always talking to my elder daughter about the importance of saving into a pension.\n女：我总是跟我大女儿讲存钱进养老金的重要性。\n【信号】always + importance 强调。女士说 I am always talking to my elder daughter about\nthe importance of saving into a pension，always 与 importance 双重强调，且对象\nelder daughter 与题干完全一致，这句即答案落点。\n【替换】the importance of saving into a pension ↔ The necessity of saving into a pension\nimportance 换成 necessity\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThe importance of cutting down family expenses.（削减家庭开支的重要性）虽与省钱\n沾边，但录音里女士对女儿谈的是\"存养老金\"，从未谈\"削减家庭开支\"，属无中生有。\nB) 原词陷阱\nThe need to learn important lessons from her elders. 盗用了男士末句的 it's such an\nimportant lesson to learn 与选项 11 里 elder daughter 的 elder，但 from her elders\n（向长辈学）方向反了，女士是\"对女儿讲\"不是\"向长辈学\"。\n借用原文：it's such an important lesson to learn\nC) 张冠李戴\nThe delight in seeing their savings grow. 里 see savings grow 来自女士前面讲存钱罐的\nso they can see their savings grow，那是对小孩子说的，不是对大女儿谈的内容，被错\n接到本题。\n借用原文：so they can see their savings grow", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What do we learn about the man?", "options": ["A) He is a successful businessman.", "B) He reviews books on political affairs.", "C) He has published a new bestseller.", "D) He has worked in several banks."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He has published a new bestseller.\n【定位】Our guest today is John Banks, the author of the best selling new book, Rewarding Success.\n今天的嘉宾是约翰·班克斯，畅销新书《奖励成功》的作者。\n【信号】主持人开场介绍嘉宾。信号是访谈开场的身份介绍句 Our guest today is John Banks, the\nauthor of the best selling new book——主持人介绍嘉宾时点出的身份，正是人物题答案\n最常藏身处。\n【替换】the author of the best selling new book ↔ He has published a new bestseller.\n\"畅销书作者\"=\"出版了畅销书\"\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音说他是作者、教授、政治顾问，从没说他是商人；He is a successful businessman. 属\n凭\"成功\"二字（书名 Rewarding Success）联想出来的误判，文中无据。\nB) 张冠李戴\nHe reviews books on political affairs. 盗用了开场 Books in Review（节目名\"书评\"）和\nauthor of the book 里的 book 字样，骗\"听到 book/review 就选\"。但评书的是主持人，\n他是被采访的作者，身份张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Welcome to Books in Review\nD) 原词陷阱\nHe has worked in several banks. 借的是嘉宾姓 John Banks 里的 Banks（人名）和后文\n谈经济、税收的氛围，骗人误以为他在银行工作过。实际他干的是 worked as a political\nconsultant，与银行无关。\n借用原文：John Banks", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the woman say is the most controversial?", "options": ["A) The man's ideas about education.", "B) The man's academic background.", "C) The man's attempts at drawing public attention.", "D) The man's proposals to solve economic problems."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The man's ideas about education.\n【定位】You discuss three of those problems in the book, improving public education, reducing our\nnation's healthcare burden, and increasing personal savings. But your ideas about education\nare the most controversial.\n你在书里谈了其中三个问题：改善公共教育、减轻国家医疗负担、增加个人储蓄。但你关于\n教育的主张最具争议。\n【信号】转折 But + 最高级 the most controversial。信号是女方话里的转折加最高级 But your\nideas about education are the most controversial.——But 之后才是她真正想强调的落\n点，the most controversial 直接点名争议所在。\n【替换】your ideas about education are the most controversial ↔ The man's ideas about education.\n近乎原句，只换 your→The man's\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nThe man's academic background. 盗用了前文 let's talk about your background 里的\nbackground。但谈背景是 But 之前的铺垫闲聊，女方明确说\"最有争议\"的是教育主张，不\n是背景。\n借用原文：let's talk about your background\nC) 偷换概念\nThe man's attempts at drawing public attention. 借了前文 why it's getting so much\nattention 里的 attention。原文 attention 指的是\"书为何受关注\"，并非说他\"刻意博关\n注\"，是把客观受关注偷换成主观博眼球。\n借用原文：why it's getting so much attention\nD) 偷换概念\nThe man's proposals to solve economic problems. 沾了全书谈经济问题的大背景。但女\n方点名最具争议的是 education（教育）这一具体领域，不是笼统的\"经济提案\"，范围对不\n上。\n借用原文：problems in our country", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the man say is his real goal?", "options": ["A) To reduce students' financial burden.", "B) To motivate all students to be successful.", "C) To give students incentive to excel in economics.", "D) To provide remedies for students' poor performance."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） To motivate all students to be successful.\n【定位】A lot of people think I'm trying to punish students who aren't doing well, when actually my\ngoal is to give all students more incentive to succeed.\n很多人以为我想惩罚成绩不好的学生，其实我的目标是给所有学生更多动力去取得成功。\n【信号】actually + my goal is 的澄清句。信号是他亲口纠正误解的句子 when actually my goal\nis to give all students more incentive to succeed——actually 推翻前面\"人们以为\"的误\n解，my goal is 直接交代真实目的，正是 real goal 题的答案落点。\n【替换】to give all students more incentive to succeed ↔ To motivate all students to be successful.\ngive incentive=motivate，succeed=be successful\n【排除】A) 未提及\nTo reduce students' financial burden. 谈减轻经济负担，但他在第 6 段说的目的是\"给学\n生动力\"，与减负无关；减负是另一个层面的话题，文中没把它当作他的目标。\nC) 原词陷阱\nTo give students incentive to excel in economics. 几乎照搬原话 give … incentive，却\n把 all students to succeed 偷偷缩成 excel in economics（在经济学上拔尖）。他要激励\n的是所有学生取得成功，不限于经济这一科。\n借用原文：to give all students more incentive to succeed\nD) 与原文相反\nTo provide remedies for students' poor performance. 借了女方后文 poor\nperformance in school 的原词，听着像\"针对差生补救\"。但他的目的恰恰不是只盯差生\n——这正是他要澄清反对的\"惩罚差生\"那层误解的变体。\n借用原文：poor performance in school isn't a child's fault", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What will be one option for funding the proposed rewards according to the man?", "options": ["A) Improving school budgeting.", "B) Increasing tuition fees.", "C) Seeking donations.", "D) Raising taxes."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Raising taxes.\n【定位】Well, by increasing taxes or moving money from other areas of the budget into education.\n嗯，靠加税，或者把预算中其他领域的钱挪到教育上来。\n【信号】对被问者\"钱怎么出\"的直接应答。信号是被问方对女方提问 How will we fund the\nrewards in the meantime? 的直接应答 by increasing taxes or moving money from\nother areas——对话题里问题落在哪、答案就在被问者那句应答里。\n【替换】by increasing taxes ↔ Raising taxes.\nincrease taxes=raise taxes\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nImproving school budgeting. 盗用了原话 moving money from other areas of the\nbudget 里的 budget。但他说的是\"把预算里别处的钱挪进教育\"，不是\"改善学校的预算编\n制\"，做法被偷换。\n借用原文：moving money from other areas of the budget into education\nB) 未提及\nIncreasing tuition fees. 与正确项 Raising taxes. 共用 increase/raise 结构、都谈\"加钱\"，\n极易混。但他全程没提涨学费，只说加税和挪预算，tuition 是凭\"学校、学生\"话题脑补的。\nC) 未提及\nSeeking donations. 即募集捐款，录音里他只给了加税和挪预算两条路，从没提过募捐，\n属凭\"筹钱\"话题硬补的选项。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "Why is the speaker launching a campaign?", "options": ["A) To show his sympathy towards people always being busy.", "B) To prevent people from complaining about being \"busy\".", "C) To call on busy people to take care of themselves.", "D) To help busy people to sort out their priorities."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （目的题） To prevent people from complaining about being \"busy\".\n【定位】So I'm launching a campaign to stop people complaining about being \"busy.\"\n所以我要发起一场运动，让大家别再抱怨『忙』了。\n【信号】to 引出意图，紧跟在 campaign 后。答案句里 campaign 后面紧跟一个 to stop people\ncomplaining，这个不定式直接交代了发起运动的目的——听到 launching a campaign\nto… 时，to 后面那一截就是答案落点，这是目的题最典型的信号。\n【替换】to stop people complaining about being \"busy.\" ↔\nTo prevent people from complaining about being \"busy\".\nstop = prevent，近义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 项 To show his sympathy towards people always being busy. 利用了原文 you being\nbusy doesn't make me sympathetic at all 里的 sympathetic 一词，但原话是『一点也不\n让我同情』，说话人恰恰是不同情，A 把否定听成了肯定、方向反了。\n借用原文：you being busy doesn't make me sympathetic at all\nC) 未提及\nC 项 To call on busy people to take care of themselves. 说发起运动是为了呼吁忙人照顾\n好自己，但全文从未提到 take care of themselves，说话人发起运动是为了让人别再抱怨\n忙，而非关心忙人的健康，属凭常识臆造。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项 To help busy people to sort out their priorities. 借了原文 it's just about priorities\n里的 priorities 一词，但那句是在第四段解释『没人真的太忙、只是优先级问题』，并不是\n发起运动的目的；D 把后文一个无关概念词搬来充当发起动机，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：nobody is too busy—it's just about priorities", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the speaker advise us to do next time someone asks how we are?", "options": ["A) Avoid saying we are busy.", "B) Reflect on their true purpose.", "C) Describe our schedule in detail.", "D) Respond as most busy people do."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Avoid saying we are busy.\n【定位】So the next time someone asks you how you are, maybe respond differently.\n所以下次有人问你『最近怎么样』，也许可以换个方式回答。\n【信号】next time + maybe，建议信号。答案句以 So the next time someone asks you how\nyou are 起头、接 maybe respond differently，next time 与建议词 maybe 连用，正是\n说话人给建议的标志句——听到 maybe / you should / next time 一类软建议词，后面那\n句就是建议内容。\n【替换】respond differently ↔ Avoid saying we are busy.\ndifferently 的具体所指 = 别再说忙\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 项 Reflect on their true purpose. 借了原文反复出现的 purpose（their real purpose）\n一词，但说话人在第四段讲 real purpose 是在解释『忙是借口』，并没有建议我们『去反\n思真正目的』；B 把别处的概念词搬来当本题建议，答非所问。\n借用原文：they're not actually accomplishing their real purpose\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项 Describe our schedule in detail. 用了原文出现过的 schedule（YOUR spot on their\nschedule），但说话人从没建议我们详细描述自己的日程，反而是在反对把『忙』挂嘴边；\nC 借 schedule 一词编了个原文没给的建议。\n借用原文：it's a reflection of YOUR spot on their schedule\nD) 与原文相反\nD 项 Respond as most busy people do. 说『像多数忙人那样回答』，但原文恰恰让你\nrespond differently（换个方式、不要照旧），D 取了与建议完全相反的方向，正话反说。\n借用原文：maybe respond differently", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "Why do many people make the \"I'm busy\" response according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) To show their achievements resulted from great efforts.", "B) To prove they stand out as accomplished professionals.", "C) To cover up their failure to achieve some purpose.", "D) To tell others a complete lie about their inability."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） To cover up their failure to achieve some purpose.\n【定位】In my experience, the \"I'm busy\" response is really covering up the fact that they're not\nactually accomplishing their real purpose.\n以我的经验，『我很忙』这种回答其实是在掩盖一个事实——他们并没有真正完成自己真正\n的目标。\n【信号】really + covering up，点出真实原因。答案句里 is really covering up the fact 的 really\n（其实/真正）是揭示真相的强调信号，covering up（掩盖）直接说出动机——听到 really\n/ actually / the truth is 这类点真相的词，后面就是说话人要你听到的真实原因。\n【替换】covering up the fact that they're not actually accomplishing their real purpose ↔\nTo cover up their failure to achieve some purpose.\n没做成 = failure，概念升级\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 项 To show their achievements resulted from great efforts. 用了与原文\naccomplished 同根的 achievements，但原文 \"being busy\" doesn't…mean…\naccomplished 明说忙并不等于有成就，说话人是在否定『忙=有成就』，A 却说人们这么\n答是为了炫耀成就，方向反了。\n借用原文：\"being busy\" doesn't stand out, nor does it mean productive, creative, accomplished,\nor professional\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 项 To prove they stand out as accomplished professionals. 同时搬了原文 stand\nout、accomplished、professional 三个原词，迷惑性很强，但原文那句是说『忙并不让人\n显得突出、也不代表专业』，是否定；B 反过来说人们答忙是为了证明自己是杰出专业人\n士，与原意相反、也不是题目问的真实动机。\n借用原文：\"being busy\" doesn't stand out, nor does it mean productive, creative, accomplished,\nor professional\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项 To tell others a complete lie about their inability. 借了原文 the lie they tell\nthemselves 的 lie，但原文说的是『他们对自己撒的谎』、且没有 complete（彻底）这种\n程度，D 改成『对别人说彻底的谎、关于自己的无能』，对象（自己→别人）和程度（a\nlie→a complete lie）都被篡改、过度夸大。\n借用原文：\"being busy\" is the lie they tell themselves about why they can't achieve it", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What may sound strange to say about extreme sports?", "options": ["A) They will help one reduce fear", "B) They should become so popular.", "C) They will be less challenging after a time.", "D) They should cover so many different types."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They will help one reduce fear\n【定位】It may sound strange to say that their main function is to reduce fear.\n要说极限运动的主要作用是减少恐惧，这听起来也许有点奇怪。\n【信号】题干原话 sound strange 在原文回响。原文 It may sound strange to say that their\nmain function is to reduce fear 把题干里的 may sound strange to say 几乎原样念出，\n这种\"题干措辞在原文重现\"是最强定位信号，紧跟其后的 to reduce fear 即答案内容。\n【替换】their main function is to reduce fear ↔ They will help one reduce fear\nmain function = will help，reduce fear 原义保留\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n第1段确实念到 Why are these sports becoming so popular?，They should become so\npopular 把 popular 原词搬来；但\"流行\"是全文引子，并非那句\"听起来奇怪\"的说法，属听\n到原词就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：Why are these sports becoming so popular?\nC) 未提及\nThey will be less challenging after a time 听起来\"奇怪\"但录音从未说极限运动会\"变得不\n那么有挑战\"；它蹭了第2段 after a time 这个时间词，却把内容编造成\"难度下降\"，属未提\n及。\n借用原文：after a time, your fear response to other things in life will be dramatically reduced\nD) 张冠李戴\n第1段列举 snowboarding, skateboarding, mountain biking, and many more，They\nshould cover so many different types 借此说\"涵盖很多类型\"；但这是开篇举例，不是题\n干所指那句\"听起来奇怪\"的话，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：snowboarding, skateboarding, mountain biking, and many more", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "Why should one be highly focused when doing extreme sports?", "options": ["A) To break one's ultimate limits.", "B) To stop being extremely afraid.", "C) To avoid dangerous mistakes.", "D) To enjoy the sports to the full."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） To avoid dangerous mistakes.\n【定位】You'll be pushed to your limits, and if you aren't focused you'll make dangerous mistakes.\n你会被逼到极限，如果不专注，就会犯危险的错误。\n【信号】if 引出的因果条件句。if you aren't focused you'll make dangerous mistakes 是个 if 条\n件句：\"不专注→犯危险错误\"，反过来即\"专注是为了避免危险错误\"。if…you'll… 的因果结\n构就是原因题的答案信号。\n【替换】if you aren't focused you'll make dangerous mistakes ↔ To avoid dangerous mistakes\n肯否转换：不专注就犯错 = 专注为避免犯错\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文 You'll be pushed to your limits 念到 limits，To break one's ultimate limits 抢用\nlimits 制造\"听到就选\"；但原文是\"你会被逼到极限\"，并非\"专注是为了突破极限\"，属原词\n陷阱。\n借用原文：You'll be pushed to your limits\nB) 张冠李戴\nTo stop being extremely afraid 对应的是第2段\"减少恐惧\"的内容，与第3段\"为什么要专\n注\"无关；把别段的好处安到本题，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：their main function is to reduce fear\nD) 未提及\nTo enjoy the sports to the full 听着合理，但录音从没说\"专注是为了把运动玩到尽兴\"；它\n无原文出处，是凭常识编出来的，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "How can extreme sports benefit us more than standard exercise routines and sports?", "options": ["A) By allowing our motivation to be at an all-time high.", "B) By stopping us hurting the same muscles repeatedly.", "C) By burning as many as 300 calories per hour.", "D) By enabling us to get an all-over workout."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） By enabling us to get an all-over workout.\n【定位】With extreme sports, you'll be working entirely different muscles, and that means you get an\nall-over workout.\n做极限运动时，你会动用到完全不同的肌肉，这意味着你能得到全身锻炼。\n【信号】对比 + that means 引出结论。前句 standard exercise routines and sports only work\nthe same muscles repeatedly 与本句\"动用完全不同肌肉\"形成对比，that means you get\nan all-over workout 用 that means 把结论点出——\"对比 + that means 的结论\"就是答\n案信号。\n【替换】that means you get an all-over workout ↔ By enabling us to get an all-over workout\nthat means = By enabling us to，all-over workout 原义保留\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nBy allowing our motivation to be at an all-time high 盗用第5段 your self-confidence\nwill be at an all-time high 的 all-time high；但原文说的是\"自信\"达到最高、且与\"比常规\n运动好在哪\"无关，且把 self-confidence 偷换成 motivation，原词陷阱兼偷换概念。\n借用原文：your self-confidence will be at an all-time high\nB) 与原文相反\n原文说常规运动 only work the same muscles repeatedly（反复练同一块肌肉，是它的不\n足）；By stopping us hurting the same muscles repeatedly 盗用 same muscles\nrepeatedly，却把它说成极限运动的益处方式，与原文指向相反。\n借用原文：only work the same muscles repeatedly\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文 Basketball burns around 300 in the same time，300 是篮球的消耗；极限运动（滑\n板）是 can burn as many as 500 calories per hour。By burning as many as 300\ncalories per hour 故意配错数字，是典型数字陷阱。\n借用原文：Basketball burns around 300 in the same time", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the passage say about conflict in organizations?", "options": ["A) It is natural.", "B) It is instrumental.", "C) It is personal.", "D) It is vital."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It is natural.\n【定位】Conflict is natural. We all bring different life and work experiences to the table.\n冲突是自然的。我们每个人都把各自不同的生活和工作经历带到一起来。\n【信号】is + 形容词的直接定性。答案信号是一句最直白的判断句 Conflict is natural.——主语正是\n题问的 conflict，紧跟 is + 形容词给它定性，这种'X is + 形容词'的下定义句就是定性题的\n落点。\n【替换】Conflict is natural. ↔ It is natural.\n代词替换+原词保留\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIt is instrumental.（起工具/促进作用的）原文从未用来形容冲突，是凭空安上的形容词，\n与定性句 Conflict is natural. 不符。\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文后文确实提到 We all have different personality preferences and tendencies.，It is\npersonal. 盗用了 personality 这个原词，让人觉得'听到了'。但那句是在解释冲突为何自\n然，并非给冲突定性为'个人的'，属答非所问。\n借用原文：We all have different personality preferences and tendencies.\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt is vital. 盗用的是第 3 段 Productive conflict is a vital part of teams and\norganizations 里的 vital，但那是形容'有益的冲突 productive conflict'，而本题问的是冲\n突本身的性质，原文这里给的定性是 natural，不是 vital。\n借用原文：Productive conflict is a vital part of teams and organizations", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "Why do some people want to avoid conflict at all costs?", "options": ["A) To guard against being perceived negatively.", "B) To prevent themselves from being isolated.", "C) To conceal personality preferences.", "D) To maintain workplace harmony."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） To maintain workplace harmony.\n【定位】But too often we get caught in this place where conflict is perceived to be negative—\nsomething we want to avoid so that we can maintain the harmony of our workplace.\n但我们常常陷入这样一种境地：冲突被视为负面的东西——一种我们想要回避的东西，以便\n能维持工作场所的和谐。\n【信号】so that 引出目的。答案信号是表目的的连接词 so that：something we want to avoid\nso that we can maintain the harmony of our workplace，so that 之后正是'回避冲突'的\n目的，这就是 Why 题要的落点。\n【替换】so that we can maintain the harmony of our workplace ↔ To maintain workplace harmony.\n语序压缩+同词复现\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTo guard against being perceived negatively. 盗用了原文 conflict is perceived to be\nnegative 里的 perceived/negative，听起来很耳熟。但原文那句是说'冲突被视为负面'，\n是回避的背景，不是人们回避冲突所追求的目的，属答非所问。\n借用原文：conflict is perceived to be negative\nB) 未提及\nTo prevent themselves from being isolated.（防止自己被孤立）原文从未提到'被孤立'，\n是凭空添加的目的，与 so that 后的'维持和谐'无关。\nC) 原词陷阱\nTo conceal personality preferences. 盗用第 1 段 We all have different personality\npreferences and tendencies. 里的 personality preferences，但那是讲冲突为何自然，\n跟'回避冲突的目的'毫不相干，且原文没有'隐藏 conceal'之意。\n借用原文：We all have different personality preferences and tendencies.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "Why is productive conflict important for teams and organizations?", "options": ["A) It helps to enhance team spirit.", "B) It stimulates innovative ideas.", "C) It helps to resolve problems.", "D) It facilitates policy-making."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It stimulates innovative ideas.\n【定位】Without conflict, we're often stuck in this artificial harmony where people don't express\npotentially innovative ideas for fear that they may start conflict with others.\n没有冲突，我们往往困在这种虚假的和谐里，人们由于害怕会与他人引发冲突，而不去表达\n那些可能很有创新性的想法。\n【信号】Without...反面论证。答案信号是 Without conflict 引出的反面假设：没有冲突，人们就不\n敢说出 potentially innovative ideas。反过来即说明'有益的冲突'的价值就在于能让创新想\n法被表达出来，这是从反面点出作用的落点。\n【替换】people don't express potentially innovative ideas ↔ It stimulates innovative ideas.\n否定反推+同词保留\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt helps to enhance team spirit. 借了原文反复出现的 team(s)，让人一听 team 就想选。\n但原文说冲突带来的是 innovation 与 trust，从未说它增强'团队精神 team spirit'，属答非\n所问。\n借用原文：greater trust on teams\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt helps to resolve problems. 利用第 1 段 how to approach policies, programs or\nproblems 里的 problems 一词，但那是说大家对如何处理问题有分歧（冲突的来源），并\n非说冲突能'解决问题'，是偷换概念。\n借用原文：how to approach policies, programs or problems\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt facilitates policy-making. 把第 1 段 approach policies, programs or problems 中的\npolicies 改头换面成 policy-making，但原文从未说冲突有助于'制定政策'，是无中生有的\n作用。\n借用原文：how to approach policies, programs or problems", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does productive conflict need as a basis?", "options": ["A) An innovative mind.", "B) Corporate culture.", "C) Mutual trust.", "D) A healthy mentality."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Mutual trust.\n【定位】But if you're in a place where you have a basis of trust, conflict can be extremely productive.\n但如果你处在一个有信任基础的环境里，冲突可以变得极富成效。\n【信号】a basis of 直接点前提。答案信号就是题干同款词 basis：if you're in a place where you\nhave a basis of trust，a basis of 后面紧跟的 trust 正是冲突变得有益所需的基础，与设问\n的 as a basis 直接对上。\n【替换】a basis of trust ↔ Mutual trust.\n核心词同+补足限定\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nAn innovative mind. 盗用原文多次出现的 innovative/innovation，但创新在原文里是冲\n突带来的'结果'（lead to increased innovation），不是它需要的'基础'，前提与结果被颠\n倒。\n借用原文：It can lead to increased innovation\nB) 未提及\nCorporate culture.（企业文化）原文从未提及，是借'谈职场就该有企业文化'的常识硬塞\n的，与 basis of trust 无关。\nD) 原词陷阱\nA healthy mentality. 盗用原文 good leaders and healthy teams 里的 healthy，但那是\n形容'健康的团队'，而非冲突所需的'健康心态 mentality'，原文也没说前提是某种心态，是\n张冠李戴。\n借用原文：good leaders and healthy teams", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) attaining", "C) conclusion", "D) difficult", "E) emerges", "F) fixed", "G) ignored", "H) legally", "I) manipulated", "J) recommended", "K) refraining", "L) signals", "M) significantly", "N) source", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) ultimate\n形容词\n【词性槽】and 前后并列，名词 purpose 之前需要一个与 broader 平行的形容词\n【依据】ultimate purpose 是英语固定搭配，意为「最终目的」\nthe ultimate purpose of action「行动的最终目的」与 the broader picture「更宏观的画\n面」语义共振，都指向抽象 speech 关注「全局/根本」。与 abstract speech that focuses\non the broader picture and __(26)__ purpose of action 的语境严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】D) difficult difficult purpose 搭配生硬、语义跑偏\ndifficult 表「困难的」，「困难的行动目的」既不是固定搭配，也与「抽象 speech 关注大局」\n的语义无关。留给真正讲「难以…」的空位（30 题）。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) attaining", "C) conclusion", "D) difficult", "E) emerges", "F) fixed", "G) ignored", "H) legally", "I) manipulated", "J) recommended", "K) refraining", "L) signals", "M) significantly", "N) source", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) attaining\n动词\n【词性槽】介词 of 之后需要动名词作宾语\n【依据】与前句的 purpose of action 构成「目的—手段」对照\n前一句讲抽象 speech 关注 the broader picture and __(26)__ purpose of action，本句讲\n具体 speech 关注 details and the means of __(27)__ action。purpose of action（行动\n的目的）与 means of attaining action（达成行动的手段）正好构成「为什么做—怎么\n做」的对偶，attaining action 意为「实现行动」，逻辑严密。\n【竞争词】K) refraining 搭配硬伤：refrain 是不及物动词，必须接 from\nrefrain 表「克制、避免」时，固定搭配是 refrain from doing sth.，不能直接接名词宾语\naction；且语义上「克制行动的手段」与「具体讲细节」无关，逻辑也跑偏。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) attaining", "C) conclusion", "D) difficult", "E) emerges", "F) fixed", "G) ignored", "H) legally", "I) manipulated", "J) recommended", "K) refraining", "L) signals", "M) significantly", "N) source", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) conclusion\n名词\n【词性槽】指示代词 this 之后需要名词\n【依据】arrive at a conclusion 是固定搭配，意为「得出结论」\n上一段 they found that men were far more likely to speak in the abstract than were\nwomen 已经摆出了研究结论；本段开头 To arrive at this __(28)__, the researchers\nexamined…「为得出这一结论，研究者们考察了…」，回指上段的研究发现，conclusion\n是英语阅读里 arrive at 最高频的宾语之一。\n【竞争词】N) source\n搭配错位且语义反向：arrive at this source 不通，且 source 强调「源头」与此处「结\n论」反向\narrive at a source 不是英语习惯搭配；语义上 source 指「根源/来源」，应该是「为查明这一\n现象的根源」才用 source——而那正是 32 题要做的事。把 source 留给 32 题，conclusion 留\n给 28 题，分工干净。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) attaining", "C) conclusion", "D) difficult", "E) emerges", "F) fixed", "G) ignored", "H) legally", "I) manipulated", "J) recommended", "K) refraining", "L) signals", "M) significantly", "N) source", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) approximately\n副词\n【词性槽】空后紧跟数词，需要修饰数词的副词\n【依据】approximately 是英语里专门修饰数词表「大约」的标配副词\napproximately + 数字 是英语里几乎固定的表达「大约 40,000 个常用词」，对应中文「研\n究者对大约 4 万个常用英语单词计算了抽象度评分」，与 computed abstractness ratings\nfor __(29)__ 40,000 commonly used words 完全贴合。\n【竞争词】H) legally 语义跑偏：合法地 ≠ 大约\nlegally 表「合法地、在法律上」，与「4 万个单词」的语境无关——研究者不是「合法地对\n40,000 个单词评分」，纯粹是中文译过去也读不通的错配。\nM) significantly significantly 修饰程度/动作，不修饰具体数词\nsignificantly 表「显著地」，一般修饰动词或形容词（如 used significantly more /\nsignificantly higher），不与具体数字 40,000 直接搭配。这一项要留给后面真正涉及「显著多/\n显著高」的空。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) attaining", "C) conclusion", "D) difficult", "E) emerges", "F) fixed", "G) ignored", "H) legally", "I) manipulated", "J) recommended", "K) refraining", "L) signals", "M) significantly", "N) source", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) difficult\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词 were 之后作表语\n【依据】与上一句「easily visualized」形成 easy ↔ difficult 的反义对照\n前一句说具体词「Words considered to be concrete could be easily visualized, such as\ntable or chair」（容易在脑中可视化）；本句说抽象词「more difficult to visualize, for\nexample, justice or morality」（更难可视化）。easy ↔ difficult 的对照轴是命题人埋的\n明信号。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) attaining", "C) conclusion", "D) difficult", "E) emerges", "F) fixed", "G) ignored", "H) legally", "I) manipulated", "J) recommended", "K) refraining", "L) signals", "M) significantly", "N) source", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) significantly\n副词\n【词性槽】动词 used 与比较级 more abstract language 之间需要副词修饰\n【依据】significantly more 是研究/统计文体的高频搭配「显著更多」\nresearch / statistics 文体里 significantly more / significantly higher 几乎是标配表达\n「在统计上显著高于」，与 They found that men used __(31)__ more abstract\nlanguage 强调「男性使用抽象语言显著多于女性」的研究主旨完全吻合。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) attaining", "C) conclusion", "D) difficult", "E) emerges", "F) fixed", "G) ignored", "H) legally", "I) manipulated", "J) recommended", "K) refraining", "L) signals", "M) significantly", "N) source", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) source\n名词\n【词性槽】定冠词 the 之后、of 之前需要名词\n【依据】the source of this effect 意为「这一效应的根源」，正是下文要追究的「为何如此」\n紧接着 The researchers suggest that power differences between the genders…might\nbe a key determinant——把权力差异当作「关键决定因素」，正是回答「root/source 在\n哪里」的问题。source「根源」与 determinant「决定因素」语义共振。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) attaining", "C) conclusion", "D) difficult", "E) emerges", "F) fixed", "G) ignored", "H) legally", "I) manipulated", "J) recommended", "K) refraining", "L) signals", "M) significantly", "N) source", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) manipulated\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 the researchers 之后需要谓语动词（过去式）\n【依据】manipulate 在心理学实验语境里的固定义：操控/调控自变量\n实验研究中 manipulate 是术语：研究者「人为操控/调控」某一变量看其因果效应。原文\nin a follow-up study conducted with a sample of 300 students, the researchers\n__(33)__ power dynamics in an interpersonal setting to see if this would influence\ncommunicative abstraction——研究者「操控了人际情境中的权力动态」，正是典型的实\n验设计描述。\n【竞争词】G) ignored 逻辑反向：研究的就是 power dynamics，怎么会去「忽视」它\nignored「忽视」与本句目的「to see if this would influence…」（看它是否影响…）正相反\n——研究者是要观察权力变量的作用，不可能去忽视它。\nJ) recommended 搭配不当：recommend「推荐」与权力动态不搭\nrecommend 用于「向某人推荐某物/做法」，把 power dynamics 当作被推荐物在语义上不\n通，也不是实验研究术语。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 G) —— ignored 词形完全合规，靠中文直觉容易选——但因果与\n下文反向。ignored 与 manipulated 都是过去式及物动词，初看「忽视权力动态」似乎也\n能讲。但本句目的状语 to see if this would influence communicative abstraction 表明\n研究者要观察其作用，逻辑上必须「调控/操作」它而非「忽视」它。防坑口诀：动词空务\n必把目的/结果状语读完再下手。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) attaining", "C) conclusion", "D) difficult", "E) emerges", "F) fixed", "G) ignored", "H) legally", "I) manipulated", "J) recommended", "K) refraining", "L) signals", "M) significantly", "N) source", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) fixed\n形容词\n【词性槽】不定冠词 a 与名词 tendency 之间是定语位\n【依据】与下半句 rather…within specific contexts 形成「固有 vs 因情境而异」的对比\n原句结构 does not reflect a __ tendency…but rather __ within specific contexts 是 not\nA but B 的反义对比。后半 within specific contexts（在具体情境中浮现）暗示「不是与生\n俱来、不变的」，对偶位填 fixed「固定的、固有的」最贴：「不是男女固有的倾向，而是\n在特定情境中浮现」。\n【竞争词】D) difficult 语义不沾：困难的倾向？讲不通\ndifficult tendency「困难的倾向」既不符合搭配习惯，也与「不是…而是…在情境中浮现」的反\n义结构不对偶。\nO) ultimate\n义近但方向错：ultimate 强调「终极/根本」，但本句要的是「固有不变」的对照\nultimate tendency 字面像「根本倾向」，单看半句似乎也通。但它无法与 within specific\ncontexts「在具体情境中」构成清晰反义；真正的对照只能是「固定不变 vs 因情境而异」，故\nfixed 比 ultimate 更精准——而且 ultimate 已经在 26 题与 purpose 搭配用掉了，依「用过即\n划」也不能再用。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 O) —— ultimate tendency 看着也通——其实 fixed 才与 within\nspecific contexts 构成反义。主体考生最常踩的坑：抓住「ultimate」的「最终的、根本\n的」译义后觉得 ultimate tendency「根本倾向」也讲得通。问题是 ultimate 与紧随其后\n的 but rather __(35)__ within specific contexts 构不成清晰反义，而 fixed「固定的、固有\n的」恰好与「在具体情境中浮现」对偶。防坑口诀：not A but B 句式里，A 的词义必须能\n从 B 反推出来。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) attaining", "C) conclusion", "D) difficult", "E) emerges", "F) fixed", "G) ignored", "H) legally", "I) manipulated", "J) recommended", "K) refraining", "L) signals", "M) significantly", "N) source", "O) ultimate"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) emerges\n动词\n【词性槽】but rather 后并列谓语，需与前句 reflect 平行的第三人称单数动词\n【依据】与 within specific contexts「在具体情境中」最自然搭配——浮现、出现\nemerge within/in specific contexts「在特定情境中浮现」是地道搭配，对应「这种抽象表\n达倾向是因情境而生」的结论，与 does not reflect a __(34)__ fixed tendency（不是固有\n倾向）正好对仗。\n【竞争词】L) signals signals 是及物动词，需要宾语，不能裸接 within…\nsignals 作动词时意为「表明、预示」，常接宾语（如 signals a change）。这里空后直接跟介\n词短语 within specific contexts，没有宾语，搭配上就走不通；且语义「表明在情境中」也读\n不顺。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Our brains are genetically determined to satisfy immediate desires.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】脑天生求即时满足\n【定位】Our brains are hard-wired for instant gratification.\n我们的大脑天生就被设定为追求即时满足。\n【改写】Our brains are genetically determined ↔ Our brains are hard-wired\n近义替换：基因决定=硬接线\nsatisfy immediate desires ↔ instant gratification\n名词化对应：满足即时欲望=即时满足\n【辨邻】 E we prioritize our current mood over the consequences of our inaction for the\nfuture self 描述的是选择偏好，并未说明这种偏好是“基因决定”或“天生写死”，没有对\n应 genetically determined 这一关键定性。\nwe prioritize our current mood over the consequences of our inaction for the future self", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Taken in a practical way, saving for post-work years is like giving money away to others.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】存退休金=送钱给别人\n【定位】Understood in that way, saving for retirement is the equivalent of giving money away to\nsomeone else entirely.\n这样一想，为退休存钱就等同于把钱完全送给另一个人。\n【改写】saving for post-work years ↔ saving for retirement\n上位/同义替换：退休=工作以后的岁月\nis like giving money away to others ↔\nis the equivalent of giving money away to someone else entirely\n同义改写：is like = is the equivalent of\n【辨邻】 B participants' brain activity while considering their future selves more closely\nresembled their brain activity while thinking about a current other rather than\nthe current self 只说脑活动相似，并未把“存养老金”比作“把钱送给别人”。\nparticipants' brain activity while considering their future selves more closely resembled their brain\nactivity while thinking about a current other rather than the current self", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Research found that, as regards achievement of one's goals, it is important to focus more on enjoying the process than the long-term benefits.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】享受过程比长期好处更重要\n【定位】These findings suggest that when it comes to achieving your goals, enjoying the process itself\nis more important than wanting the long-term benefits.\n这些发现表明，谈到达成目标时，享受过程本身比追求长期好处更重要。\n【改写】focus more on enjoying the process than the long-term benefits ↔\nenjoying the process itself is more important than wanting the long-term benefits\n句式重构：focus more on X than Y = X is more important than Y\nas regards achievement of one's goals ↔ when it comes to achieving your goals\n介词短语等价：as regards = when it comes to\n【辨邻】 K enjoyment predicted people's goal persistence two months after setting the\ngoal far more than how important they rated their goal to be 谈的是“享受度比重\n要性更能预测坚持”，对立项不是“长期好处”而是“重要性”，与陈述句不完全对应。\nenjoyment predicted people's goal persistence two months after setting the goal far more than how\nimportant they rated their goal to be\nL people ate 50% more of a healthy food when directed to focus on the good\ntaste rather than the long-term health benefits 是支撑总结的具体例证，不直接陈\n述“享受过程 比 长期好处 更重要”这一论断。\npeople ate 50% more of a healthy food when directed to focus on the good taste rather than the\nlong-term health benefits", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Regarding our future selves as still being ourselves will help us make better long-term decisions.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】把未来自我当作自己→更好长期决策\n【定位】If the central problem is that we think of our future selves as other people, it follows that\ntrying to identify more closely with our future selves will encourage us to make better long-\nterm decisions.\n如果核心问题在于我们把未来的自己当成另一个人，那么试着与未来的自己更亲近，自然就\n会鼓励我们做出更好的长期决策。\n【改写】Regarding our future selves as still being ourselves ↔\ntrying to identify more closely with our future selves\n动词同义改写：把……视作=与……更亲近认同\nbetter long-term decisions ↔ better long-term decisions\n原样复现\n【辨邻】 G bridge the disconnect between the present and future selves and encourage\npeople to save more for retirement 描述具体实验目的，没有给出“把未来自我当作\n仍是自己→更好长期决策”这一抽象论断。\nbridge the disconnect between the present and future selves and encourage people to save more\nfor retirement", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Savings rates in America have dropped in recent decades even though people's life expectancy has increased.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】美国储蓄率下降+寿命变长\n【定位】Across the board, people are living longer. Logically, they'll need more money to live\ncomfortably in their post-work years. And yet, savings rates in the U.S. have gone down in\nrecent decades, not up.\n总体上，人们寿命越来越长。按理说他们退休后过得体面就需要更多钱。然而美国近几十年\n储蓄率不升反降。\n【改写】Savings rates in America have dropped in recent decades ↔\nsavings rates in the U.S. have gone down in recent decades, not up\n同义替换：drop=go down；America=the U.S.\npeople's life expectancy has increased ↔ people are living longer\n名词化改写：人活得更长=人均寿命增加", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Researchers found that enjoyment rather than importance enabled people to persist in their goals.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】享受度而非重要性使人坚持\n【定位】We found that enjoyment predicted people's goal persistence two months after setting the goal\nfar more than how important they rated their goal to be.\n我们发现，享受度比受访者对目标重要性的评分，远远更能预测两个月后是否仍在坚持\n目标。\n【改写】enjoyment rather than importance ↔\nenjoyment predicted people's goal persistence two months after setting the goal far more than\nhow important they rated their goal to be\n对照同义：rather than = far more than\nenabled people to persist in their goals ↔ predicted people's goal persistence\n动词化改写：促使人坚持=预测坚持度\n【辨邻】 M enjoying the process itself is more important than wanting the long-term\nbenefits 的对照项是“长期好处”，与陈述句中“重要性”不是同一对照，因此不是源\n头段。\nenjoying the process itself is more important than wanting the long-term benefits\nL people ate 50% more of a healthy food when directed to focus on the good\ntaste rather than the long-term health benefits 谈的是“好吃 vs 长期健康”，没有\n“重要性”的对照。\npeople ate 50% more of a healthy food when directed to focus on the good taste rather than the\nlong-term health benefits", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "When making decisions, we give priority to our current frame of mind without thinking much of the consequences.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】重当下心情、不顾后果\n【定位】We believe that we will be different tomorrow; but in doing so, we prioritize our current\nmood over the consequences of our inaction for the future self.\n我们相信明天的自己会不一样，但这样一来，我们就把当下的心情置于不作为对未来自我所\n带来的后果之上。\n【改写】we give priority to our current frame of mind ↔ we prioritize our current mood\n同义替换：give priority to=prioritize；frame of mind=mood\nwithout thinking much of the consequences ↔\nover the consequences of our inaction for the future self\n同义结构：prioritize A over B = give priority to A without thinking of B\n【辨邻】 I Our brains are hard-wired for instant gratification 把对即时回报的偏好归为生物天\n性，超出陈述句“当下心情 over 后果”的描述范围。\nOur brains are hard-wired for instant gratification", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "People ate more of a healthy food when they focused on its good taste instead of its long-term benefits.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】关注好吃多吃健康食物\n【定位】For example, people ate 50% more of a healthy food when directed to focus on the good taste\nrather than the long-term health benefits.\n举例来说，当人们被引导关注食物本身好吃，而不是长期的健康好处时，他们多吃了 50%\n的健康食物。\n【改写】ate more of a healthy food ↔ ate 50% more of a healthy food\n上位概括：去掉具体数字保留方向\nfocused on its good taste instead of its long-term benefits ↔\ndirected to focus on the good taste rather than the long-term health benefits\n同义改写：instead of = rather than\n【辨邻】 M enjoying the process itself is more important than wanting the long-term\nbenefits 是抽象论断，没提具体的“健康食物”或“50%”，不是源头段。\nenjoying the process itself is more important than wanting the long-term benefits", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "As was expected, when people thought of their present selves, their brains were observed to become more active.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】想当下自我→大脑更活跃\n【定位】Unsurprisingly, people's brains were most active when thinking about their current selves and\nleast active when thinking about a current other.\n不出所料，人们的大脑在思考当下自我时最活跃，在思考当下他人时最不活跃。\n【改写】As was expected ↔ Unsurprisingly\n同义副词：如所料=不出所料\nwhen people thought of their present selves ↔ when thinking about their current selves\n同义短语：present selves = current selves\ntheir brains were observed to become more active ↔ people's brains were most active\n比较级—最高级方向同\n【辨邻】 C saving for retirement is the equivalent of giving money away to someone else\nentirely 是把脑成像结果转译成日常比喻，没有具体描述“大脑更活跃”这一实验观察。\nsaving for retirement is the equivalent of giving money away to someone else entirely", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Researchers found that participants who saw the images of their aged selves would save more for their later years than those who didn't.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】看到衰老自我者愿多存养老金\n【定位】Participants who saw their aged selves said they would save 30% more of their salary for\nretirement than the control group.\n看到自己衰老形象的被试表示，他们愿意比对照组多存 30% 的工资作养老金。\n【改写】participants who saw the images of their aged selves ↔ Participants who saw their aged selves\n原样复现\nsave more for their later years ↔ save 30% more of their salary for retirement\n同义替换+数字弱化：later years=retirement\n【辨邻】 B scanned the brains of study participants 是用脑成像比较当下/未来自我的实验，没\n有“用 VR 看衰老自我”的设定，与陈述句锚点不符。\nscanned the brains of study participants", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author say we need to do to strengthen our willpower?", "options": ["A) Keep it under control.", "B) Apply it continuously.", "C) Learn from entrepreneurs.", "D) Aim at success determinedly."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say we need to do to strengthen our willpower? 用\nwhat...need to do 句式直接问具体做法，不含 infer/imply/mainly about，是标准的细节\n题问法。\n【拆句】What does the author say we need to do to strengthen our willpower? 主干是 what\ndoes the author say (that) we need to do，后面的 to strengthen our willpower 是\n目的状语；翻译时把目的状语提到前面：\"为了强化意志力，作者说我们需要做什么\"。无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Willpower is just like a muscle; to keep it strong you need to constantly exercise it.\n意志力就像一块肌肉；要让它保持强壮，你需要不断地锻炼它。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文说意志力本身就是 the ability to control yourself，是控制者，不是被控对象；\nKeep it under control. 把主客关系倒置。\nB) ✓ 正确\nconstantly exercise it ↔ Apply it continuously.\n预判\"持续锻炼\"与 Apply it continuously. 字面同义，是 constantly exercise it 的标准\n同义替换，无需推断。\nC) 无中生有\nwhy some entrepreneurs have greater success than others 只是引出话题的例子，\n文中并未要求读者\"向企业家学习\"以强化意志力。\nD) 张冠李戴\nstrong determination that allows you to do something difficult 说的是意志力\"能让\n人坚定追求成功\"，是它的功能；选项把功能当成强化方法，张冠李戴。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "How are almost half of our daily actions performed according to the passage?", "options": ["A) Out of habit.", "B) With determination.", "C) Like muscle building.", "D) By self-discipline."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】How are almost half of our daily actions performed according to the passage? 用\nhow 询问客观执行方式，并加 according to the passage 强调贴文回答，是细节题。\n【拆句】How are almost half of our daily actions performed according to the passage? 这是\nhow 引导的被动语态疑问句，主语是 almost half of our daily actions，谓语 are\nperformed；according to the passage 是状语。考生若不熟悉被动，需理解为\"我们日常\n行为中近一半是如何被完成的\"。\n【定位】Almost half of our daily actions are part of our habits and not decisions, so once the correct\nhabits are in place, you will automatically perform those tasks on a day-to-day basis.\n我们日常行为中近一半属于习惯而不是决定，因此一旦正确习惯到位，你就会每天自动去做\n那些事。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nare part of our habits and not decisions ↔ Out of habit.\n预判\"出于习惯\"与 Out of habit. 字面一致，是 are part of our habits 的标准同义\n替换。\nB) 与原文相反\nare part of our habits and not decisions 已明说\"不是决定\"；With determination.\n与 decision 同属\"有意识决断\"的语义场，正好被否定。\nC) 张冠李戴\nmuscle building 来自 Willpower is just like a muscle 的比喻，与 Q47 的执行方式无\n关，被借来制造干扰。\nD) 张冠李戴\nself-discipline 与 Self-control 同义，但 P5 谈的是自控的功能，不是\"日常行为如何被\n执行\"，被借来诱导。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What will help people stick to doing something constructive automatically?", "options": ["A) Practising it on a day-to-day basis.", "B) Associating it with improving health.", "C) Possessing a reasonable amount of talent.", "D) Foreseeing the desired outcome it will yield."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What will help people stick to doing something constructive automatically? 是 what\n+ 具体行为，问\"什么会帮助\"，求客观条件，属细节题。\n【拆句】What will help people stick to doing something constructive automatically? 主干\nWhat will help people (to) stick to doing something；stick to 后接动名词 doing；\nconstructive 与 automatically 分别修饰\"事\"和\"做\"。基础薄弱者要注意 stick to 不是\"棍\n子\"，而是固定搭配\"坚持\"。\n【定位】Having a valuable outcome associated with a habit will help you stick to that\nhabit permanently.\n把一个有价值的结果与某个习惯绑定，会帮助你长期坚持那个习惯。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nyou will automatically perform those tasks on a day-to-day basis 中 day-to-day\n是养成习惯后\"自动每日去做\"的结果；选项把结果当方法，因果倒置。\nB) 以偏概全\nif you have a health issue 只是说明\"有价值结果\"的一个例子；选项把例子当成普适条\n件，以偏概全。\nC) 与原文相反\nEven with an incredible amount of talent, without the discipline and motivation\nto create positive habits, it can be difficult to achieve success. 明确说光有天赋不\n够；选项却把\"有一定天赋\"当成坚持习惯的条件，与原文相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nHaving a valuable outcome associated with a habit ↔\nForeseeing the desired outcome it will yield.\n预判\"预见到有价值回报\"的语义被 Foreseeing the desired outcome it will yield. 精确\n承担，是 P4 段尾结论句的同义替换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "How does the art of self-control help us succeed?", "options": ["A) By allowing us to remain clear-headed permanently.", "B) By enabling us to alter our behaviors constantly.", "C) By enabling us to take positive actions", "D) By allowing us to avoid taking risks."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】How does the art of self-control help us succeed? 用 how 询问机制，要求贴文给出\nself-control 起作用的方式，属细节题。\n【拆句】How does the art of self-control help us succeed? 主干 How does X help us (to)\nsucceed；help 后省略 to，接动词原形 succeed。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Self-control enables you to avoid behaviors that don't contribute to your success and adopt\nthose that do.\n自控让你能够避开那些无助于成功的行为，并采纳那些有助于成功的行为。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文未提\"保持头脑清醒\"，更未说 permanently；选项凭空生造\"永远清醒\"。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文说自控让人\"避开无益行为、采纳有益行为\"，是有方向的取舍；alter our\nbehaviors constantly 暗示\"不断改来改去\"，与作者\"养成稳定好习惯\"的立场相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\nadopt those that do ↔ By enabling us to take positive actions\nSelf-control enables you to ↔ By enabling us to\n预判同时含\"避开\"与\"采纳\"两面，选项 By enabling us to take positive actions 抓住其\n中\"采纳正向行为\"的一面，是定位句的合法概括（避开 negative = 接近 positive，二者\n同向）。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文是 avoid behaviors that don't contribute to your success，避的是\"无益行为\"，\n不是 risks；\"风险\"是文中从未出现的概念。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "Why can it be difficult for us to maintain self-control?", "options": ["A) Most of us are not in the habit of exercising self-control.", "B) We may not get immediate rewards from self-control.", "C) Self-control tends to be associated with pains.", "D) Self-control only brings about small benefits."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Why can it be difficult for us to maintain self-control? 用 why 问\"难以保持自控\"的客观\n原因，答案要能在文中找到，按细节题处理。\n【拆句】Why can it be difficult for us to maintain self-control? 这是 why 引导的疑问句 + 形式\n主语句型：it 形式主语，真主语是 to maintain self-control；can 表示\"有可能、可能会\"的\n可能性，不要误译成\"能够\"。\n【定位】Because there is a delayed satisfaction associated with self-control, it can be easy to get\noff track.\n因为自控带来的是延迟的满足感，所以人很容易脱离正轨。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文反复强调正确习惯一旦养成就会 automatically perform；选项却说\"我们大多数人\n没有自控的习惯\"，与作者立场相反，且并非作者给出的难维持原因。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthere is a delayed satisfaction associated with self-control ↔\nWe may not get immediate rewards from self-control.\n预判\"回报被推迟\"被 We may not get immediate rewards from self-control. 精确承\n担，是 because 句的正反同义改写。\nC) 无中生有\n原文谈的是 delayed satisfaction，是\"满足感被推迟\"，并非 pains\"痛苦\"；作者从未把\n自控与痛苦绑定。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文说 if you work on sticking to those small positive habits...it becomes easier\nto...achieve that delayed reward，重点是\"小习惯\"带来\"延迟的大回报\"；small\nbenefits 把延迟错读成\"好处本身小\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the passage mainly discuss regarding scientific research?", "options": ["A) Its foundation.", "B) Its rewards.", "C) Its prospect.", "D) Its funding."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题）\n【判型】What does the passage mainly discuss regarding scientific research? 中的 mainly\ndiscuss 直接锁定主旨题。\n【拆句】题干主句是 What does the passage mainly discuss，regarding scientific research 是介\n词短语作状语，限定讨论的对象范围，句式平铺直叙、无陷阱。\n【选项】A) 无中生有：选项核心词在全文找不到主线对应\nIts foundation 中的 foundation（基础／根基）在 P1~P4 中找不到对应；原文一次都\n未把科研的‘基础’作为讨论对象。\nB) 以偏概全：拿某段／某句的局部当全文主旨\nwe reap the rewards from this science 中确实出现 rewards，但只是一句话铺垫，\nP2~P4 都未围绕 ‘rewards’ 展开；Its rewards 把局部一句话上升为全文主旨。\nC) 无中生有＋时态错位\nIts prospect 中的 prospect（前景／未来展望）在 P1~P4 中无对应——作者讨论的是\n‘现状’与‘应对方式’，未对科研的未来作出预测。\nD) ✓ 正确\nmost scientific research is funded by government grants, companies doing research and\ndevelopment, and non-profit foundations\n↔ Its funding\nFunding for science has changed with the times ↔ Its funding\nIts funding 中的 Its 回指 scientific research，funding 则覆盖了 P1~P4 反复讨论的资\n助来源、资助偏差、资助审慎度——是唯一覆盖四段共同主线的选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about researchers like birdwatchers and rock collectors?", "options": ["A) They have little access to government funding.", "B) They can do research with limited resources.", "C) They can do amateur work in their own fields.", "D) They have no means for large-scale research."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the passage about researchers like birdwatchers and rock\ncollectors? 询问对某一特定群体的事实陈述，是典型细节题。\n【拆句】主干是 What do we learn from the passage about researchers，like birdwatchers and\nrock collectors 是举例修饰 researchers，结构清晰、无陷阱。\n【定位】Most of us can't afford to do nuclear research as a private hobby, but birdwatchers, rock\ncollectors, and others can do real research on a limited budget.\n我们大多数人无法把核研究当成私人爱好，但观鸟人、岩石收藏者等人可以用有限的预算做\n真正的研究。\n【选项】A) 无中生有：原文未提即排除\nThey have little access to government funding 在 P2 中找不到对应陈述——原文只\n说他们自掏腰包，并未对‘是否能拿到政府资助’作判断。\nB) ✓ 正确\non a limited budget ↔ with limited resources\ncan do real research ↔ can do research\nB 项 They can do research with limited resources 把定位句 can do real research\non a limited budget 中的能力与资源条件一一对应替换，与预判完全吻合。\nC) 偷换概念：把原文措辞替换为相近但意义偏移的表达\n原文 can do real research 强调的是‘真正的研究’，而 amateur work in their own\nfields 把‘真正研究’偷换成‘业余工作’，且 in their own fields 也是原文未提的限定。\nD) 张冠李戴＋绝对化\nThey have no means for large-scale research 把 Most of us can't afford to do\nnuclear research as a private hobby 中‘多数人’的处境强行套到 ‘birdwatchers, rock\ncollectors’ 头上，并把陈述绝对化为 no means。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What would scientific studies look like in a perfect world according to the author?", "options": ["A) They would be totally unbiased.", "B) They would be independently funded.", "C) They would be responsibly conducted.", "D) They would be strictly supervised"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What would scientific studies look like in a perfect world according to the author?\n用 would look like 询问作者对一个特定情境下的事实描述。\n【拆句】主句是 What would scientific studies look like，in a perfect world 与 according to\nthe author 都是状语修饰，按情境＋来源给约束，句式无陷阱。\n【定位】In a perfect world, money wouldn't matter—all scientific studies would be\ncompletely objective.\n在一个完美的世界里，钱不会成为问题——所有的科学研究都会是完全客观的。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ncompletely objective ↔ totally unbiased\nA 项 They would be totally unbiased 用 unbiased 反向定义 objective，用 totally 替\n换 completely，与预判 ‘完全客观’ 一一对应。\nB) 偷换概念：把比较项当成主张\n原文 Drug research sponsored by the pharmaceutical(制药的) industry is more\nlikely to end up favoring the drug under consideration than studies sponsored\nby government grants or charitable organizations 仅把独立资助的研究当作偏差较\n小的对照，并未说‘完美世界里所有研究都应独立资助’。\nC) 无中生有：选项关键词在原文无对应\nThey would be responsibly conducted 中的 responsibly 在 P3 中没有对应词或近义\n概念，属凭语感想当然。\nD) 无中生有＋话题外推\nThey would be strictly supervised 引入‘严格监管’这一原文未涉及的维度。原文只谈\n‘客观’，未谈‘监管’。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the author say about companies and special interest groups?", "options": ["A) They try hard to pull down the safeguards for research.", "B) They make extra efforts to research their own products.", "C) They provide valuable resources for scientific research.", "D) They reap the most benefits from scientific research."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about companies and special interest groups? 询问作者对\n某一明确主体的具体陈述，是细节题。\n【拆句】主句是 What does the author say about companies and special interest groups?，\nabout 引导的介词短语锁定主体对象，句式简短无陷阱。\n【定位】These groups provide invaluable funding for scientific research.\n这些团体为科学研究提供了极其宝贵的资金。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反：把肯定语句逆转\n原文 science has many safeguards in place to catch instances of bias that affect\nresearch outcomes 明确指出科学有 safeguards，作者也未指控这些团体‘试图打\n掉’safeguards；A 项 They try hard to pull down the safeguards for research 直接\n与原文事实对立。\nB) 过度推断：把现象升级为意图\n原文只说企业资助的研究‘更可能偏向其产品’（more likely to end up favoring the\ndrug under consideration），并未说企业‘额外努力地’去研究自家产品；B 项 They\nmake extra efforts to research their own products 是从结果反推动机，越界。\nC) ✓ 正确\nprovide invaluable funding for scientific research ↔\nprovide valuable resources for scientific research\nC 项 They provide valuable resources for scientific research 与定位句结构、谓语、\n宾语短语高度对齐，是定位句最纯粹的同义改写。\nD) 无中生有＋绝对化\nThey reap the most benefits from scientific research 中‘获益最多’这一比较级在 P4\n中找不到任何对应陈述——原文谈他们是‘出资方’，未谈‘获益方排序’。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author think of research funded by industry or special interest groups?", "options": ["A) Its recommendations should be examined for feasibility.", "B) Its misleading results should be corrected in time.", "C) Its validity should be checked with additional care.", "D) Its hidden biases should be identified independently."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think of research funded by industry or special interest\ngroups? 询问作者对特定 research 的明确看法，属于带观点色彩的细节题，仍可单点定位\n作者评价句。\n【拆句】主句是 What does the author think of research，funded by industry or special\ninterest groups 是 research 的过去分词后置定语，相当于 the research that is funded\nby…，句式直白无陷阱。\n【定位】Meanwhile, it pays to examine studies funded by industry or special interest groups with\nextra care.\n同时，对工业界或特殊利益团体资助的研究多加小心地审视，是值得的。\n【选项】A) 无中生有＋话题偏移\n原文谈的是‘核查这类研究的结论是否可靠’，并非‘检查其建议的可行性’；Its\nrecommendations should be examined for feasibility 中 recommendations 与\nfeasibility 都是无中生有的概念。\nB) 张冠李戴：把全局机制改成对单一对象的建议\n原文 misleading results will be corrected as science proceeds 是说科学过程会自我\n纠错，而 B 项 Its misleading results should be corrected in time 把这一过程改写为\n作者对企业资助研究的具体建议，并加入 in time 这一原文未提的时限。\nC) ✓ 正确\nexamine…with extra care ↔ Its validity should be checked with additional care\nAre the results consistent with other independently funded studies? ↔ Its validity\nC 项 Its validity should be checked with additional care 把作者 ‘要带着额外的小心\n去审查这类研究’ 用被动语态、上位名词重写，与预判一一对应。\nD) 偷换概念＋强加限定\n原文 A little examination can go a long way towards identifying bias associated\nwith the funding source 只说‘审视可帮助识别偏差’，并未要求‘独立地\n（independently）识别 hidden biases’；D 项强加 hidden 与 independently 两层原\n文没说的限制。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
